[LineName "01. What the opposition is (8/10)"]
[LineDescription "Use each king move to win a little ground, then send the pawn when its path is safe."]

{Pawn on f4. Black moves first; then win. The kings face each other across h6.
Can you turn Black's first move into ground for your king?} 1... Kg7 {[%cal
Rf4f5] Black has to move, and steps aside to g7.} 2. Kg5 {Kg5! — Take the
opposition and protect your pawn. Black must give way. f5? — Black's king blocks
the f-pawn. Only a draw now.} 2... Kf7 {[%cal Rg5h6,Rg5h5,Rf4f5] Black steps
aside, in front of your pawn.} 3. Kf5 {Kf5! — Take the opposition again: the
kings face across f6. Black must give way.} 3... Ke7 {Black steps aside, but
still guards f6.} 4. Kg6 {Kg6 — f6 is guarded, so your king walks around it.}
4... Kf8 {[%cal Rg6h7,Rg6h6] Black steps in front of the pawn, on f8.} 5. Kf6
{[%also f5] Kf6! — Take the opposition again, across f7. f5 wins just as fast.}
5... Kg8 {Black steps aside, watching f7 and f8.} 6. Ke7 {Ke7 — Your king guards
the pawn's path: f6, f7 and f8.} 6... Kg7 {[%cal Re7e8] Black comes back to
guard f6, f7 and f8.} 7. f5 {f5! — Now the pawn moves: your king already guards
the squares ahead.} 7... Kh6 {[%cal Re7e8,Re7f8] Black tries to reach your pawn
from the side.} 8. f6 {f6! — The pawn steps up, protected by your king.} 8...
Kg5 {[%cal Re7e8,Re7f8] Black attacks the pawn, but your king protects it.} 9.
f7 {f7! — One step from the finish; your king guards f8.} 9... Kf4 {Black is too
late to reach f8.} 10. f8=Q+ {Queen! With check, and your king protects the new
queen.} *

[Event "01. What the opposition is (9/10)"]
[Site "Endgame Classroom"]
[White "?"]
[Black "?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4k3/8/8/5K2/8/8/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[LineName "01. What the opposition is (9/10)"]
[LineDescription "Bring your king forward to secure the route before sending the c-pawn on its way."]

{[%cal Rc2c3,Rc2c4] Pawn on c2. White to move and win. Your king is far from the
pawn, and Black is ready to block its road. Should you push the pawn, or use
your king first?} 1. Ke6 {Ke6! — Take the opposition across e7, before touching
the pawn. c3? — Black answers Kd7 and holds the draw.} 1... Kd8 {[%cal Rc2c4]
Black steps aside, toward the c-file.} 2. Kd6 {Kd6! — Take the opposition again,
across d7.} 2... Kc8 {[%cal Rd6e7] Black steps in front of the pawn, on c8.} 3.
Kc6 {[%also c4] Kc6! — Opposition again, in front of your pawn. c4 wins just as
fast.} 3... Kb8 {Black steps aside again.} 4. c4 {c4 — Your king is in front, so
now the pawn moves.} 4... Ka7 {Black moves away from the c-file.} 5. c5 {c5 —
The pawn steps up, protected by your king.} 5... Ka6 {Black tries to reach your
pawn from the side.} 6. Kd7 {Kd7 — Your king guards the pawn's path: c6, c7 and
c8.} 6... Kb5 {[%cal Rd7c8,Rd7d8,Rd7c7] Black attacks your pawn. Can you save
it?} 7. c6 {c6! — The pawn steps up, protected by your king.} 7... Kc4 {Black's
king is left behind the pawn.} 8. c7 {c7 — The pawn reaches c7; your king guards
c8.} 8... Kd4 {Black cannot stop the pawn now.} 9. c8=Q {Queen! The pawn reaches
c8, and your king protects the new queen.} *

[Event "01. What the opposition is (10/10)"]
[Site "Endgame Classroom"]
[White "?"]
[Black "?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/2k5/8/8/1K2P3/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[LineName "01. What the opposition is (10/10)"]
[LineDescription "Win ground with your king first; then push the pawn at the right moment."]

{[%cal Rb4a5,Rb4b5,Rb4c4] Pawn on e4. White to move and win. The kings are
manoeuvring for the central road. Can you take the opposition, then find the
right moment to push the e-pawn?} 1. Kc5 {Kc5! — Take the opposition across c6.
Black must give way. Ka5? — Black answers Kc8 and holds the draw.} 1... Kd7
{[%cal Rc5b6,Rc5b5,Re4e5] Black steps aside, toward the e-file.} 2. Kd5 {Kd5! —
Take the opposition again, across d6, and protect your pawn. Black must give
way.} 2... Ke7 {[%cal Rd5c6,Rd5c5,Re4e5] Black steps in front of your pawn, on
e7.} 3. Ke5 {Ke5! — Once more: the kings face across e6. Black must give way.}
3... Kf7 {Black steps aside, but still guards e6.} 4. Kd6 {Kd6 — Your king goes
around and guards e5, e6 and e7.} 4... Kf6 {[%cal Rd6c7,Rd6d7,Rd6c6] Black comes
closer to your pawn.} 5. e5+ {e5+! — The pawn steps up with check, protected by
your king.} 5... Kf5 {[%cal Rd6c7,Rd6d7,Rd6e7] Black has to step out of check.}
6. e6 {e6! — The pawn steps up, still protected by your king.} 6... Kf6 {[%cal
Rd6c7,Rd6c6] Black attacks the pawn and guards e7.} 7. e7 {[%also Kd7] e7! — The
pawn reaches the 7th rank, protected by your king. Kd7 wins just as fast.} 7...
Kf7 {[%cal Re7e8] Black guards e8, the promotion square.} 8. Kd7 {Kd7! — Your
king guards e8 too, so the pawn can promote.} 8... Kf6 {Black steps away;
nothing can stop the pawn now.} 9. e8=Q {Queen! The pawn reaches e8, and your
king protects the new queen.} *

[Event "02. How to take the opposition (1/15)"]
[Site "Endgame Classroom"]
[White "?"]
[Black "?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2k5/8/8/2K5/8/8/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[LineName "02. How to take the opposition (1/15)"]
[LineDescription "Straight ahead: face the black king with one square between."]

{[%cal Rc2c4] Now you find the move that takes the opposition. It is not always
straight ahead: sometimes it is a diagonal step, sometimes a step to the side.
Pawn on c2. White to move and win. Take the opposition: face the black king with
one square between, and Black to move. When the kings face each other, who
should be to move? Red arrows: tempting moves that only draw.} 1. Kc6 {Kc6! —
Take the opposition. c4? — Black gets the opposition. Only a draw now.} 1... Kb8
2. c4 Ka7 3. c5 Ka6 4. Kd7 Kb5 {[%cal Rd7c8,Rd7d8,Rd7c7]} 5. c6 {c6! — Your king
protects the c-pawn.} 5... Kc4 6. c7 Kd4 7. c8=Q {Queen! Your king protects the
new queen, and the win is easy now.} *

[Event "02. How to take the opposition (2/15)"]
[Site "Endgame Classroom"]
[White "?"]
[Black "?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "6k1/8/8/7K/8/8/4P3/8 w - - 0 1"]
[LineName "02. How to take the opposition (2/15)"]
[LineDescription "When the straight step is wrong, step diagonally."]

{[%cal Re2e3,Re2e4] Pawn on e2. White to move and win. Take the opposition. The
straight step is not the right one here. When the kings face each other, who
should be to move?} 1. Kg6 {Kg6! — Take the opposition. e3? — Black answers Kf7
and holds the draw.} 1... Kf8 {[%cal Re2e4]} 2. Kf6 {Kf6! — Take the opposition
again.} 2... Kg8 3. Ke6 {[%also Ke7,e3,e4]} 3... Kg7 4. e4 Kg6 5. e5 Kg5 6. Kf7
{[%also Kd7]} 6... Kf4 {[%cal Rf7e8,Rf7f8,Rf7e7]} 7. e6 {e6! — Your king
protects the e-pawn.} 7... Ke4 8. e7 Ke5 9. e8=Q+ {Queen! Your king protects the
new queen, and the win is easy now.} *

